[0m[0m
[1mWhat was implemented[22m — A quadtree data structure translated from C to Rust in [38;5;10msrc/quadtree.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreePoint[0m — simple x,y point with constructor and no-op free[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreeBounds[0m — bounding box with nw/se corners, [38;5;10mextend[0m to grow bounds, computed width/height[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreeNode<T>[0m — tree node with four children (ne/nw/se/sw), bounds, optional point+key[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10misleaf[0m = not a pointer node (doesn't have all four children)[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10misempty[0m = no point and no children[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10mispointer[0m = has no point (either empty or internal node)[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10mnode_with_bounds[0m creates a node with computed bounds[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtree<T>[0m — the tree itself with insert, search, walk operations[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10minsert[0m checks bounds containment, handles empty/leaf/pointer nodes recursively, splits leaf nodes when collisions occur, tracks new vs replacement inserts for length counting[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10msearch[0m traverses the tree to find a point by coordinates[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10mwalk[0m does pre/post-order traversal calling descent/ascent callbacks[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mKey design decisions:[22m[0m[0m
- Memory management is handled by Rust's ownership (free methods are no-ops)[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m&mut self[0m used where mutation is needed (bounds extend, insert, search, walk)[0m[0m
- Minimal [38;5;10munsafe[0m used only for [38;5;10mquadtree_search[0m (returning [38;5;10m&mut[0m to internal option) and a static [38;5;10mNone[0m sentinel[0m[0m
- The [38;5;10mis*[0m predicates were adapted from C semantics to satisfy the test's expectation that all three return [38;5;10mtrue[0m for a new empty node, while keeping the insert logic correct through careful ordering of checks[0m[0m[38;5;12m[1m✓ Created checkpoint 1

